this week marks the first anniversary of  Donald Trump's election to the  presidency CBS News chief Washington  correspondent Face the Nation anchor  John Dickerson met some of the  president's supporters in New Hampshire  he talked with them about mr. Trump's  performance a group of three men and  three women range in ages from 26 to 82  years old they all voted for mr. Trump  last November to identify as Republicans  while the others say their independence  they spoke with John at the foundry  restaurant in Manchester about the  changes the president has made in the  country and their community it's been  one year since the aisle Trump is  elected president who here by show of  hands thinks the country is doing better  now than it was a year ago all six of  you all right Peter what what makes you  think the country is better off after a  year of Donald Trump as president well  the economy is certainly a big thing it  was nice to see the economy jump a  little bit from the end of Obama's  presidency it seems like a lot of that  is just coming from promises that Trump  is making like he's saying I'm going to  make things better for business I'm  going to lower corporate tax rates so  that builds enthusiasm so companies seem  like they're more willing to invest in  the future I agree with everything that  that Peters just said I think that the  word fiscally we're doing better  financially the stock market's going up  but I also think that it's because a lot  of things that haven't been put into  place yet like the tax reform Terry what  do you feel about people who are in  Congress should they work for their  constituents or work for Donald Trump if  they're if they're Republicans they  ought to follow their party platform and  from whatever stems from that we have a  majority or the GOP has a majority in  Congress  and the presidency and they're not  working together and it's shameful do  you think President Trump has helped  foster a sense of community in America  or oh he's made it much worse in the  past presidents have brought nations  together why is President Trump why  can't he do that  like he just well lie or like make  things up for the effect it has on other  people that's not a way that a good  person will live their life does the  president tell the truth and if he  doesn't does that bother you it does  bother and it is very frustrating it's  one of the big knocks on the president  is if he can't seem to keep his ego in  check and keep the lies in to control he  needs to act presidential and step it up  you voted for him is your vote up in the  air now absolutely  absolutely John Dickerson is with us now  this is fascinating how would you sum up  where they are well they are behind the  president in a way that I've not seen in  politics it's not just he's our guy but  the message the president is sending out  there about the economy about what he's  done in office on regulations they have  internalized they have some complaints  about him but they it's not just  boosterism it's actual belief in what he  is promoting specifically with respect  to things like tax cuts where they have  heard what he's saying and can repeat it  and are therefore it makes their support  more durable as they hear criticisms  from elites from the press from  Democrats even from other Republicans is  there a difference between those who  support Trump because of style and  culture because of the finger of the  establishment and those who find that  the policies that he talks about tax  reform how he feels about immigrants and  and other issues of those that they  fervently feel passion about there's a  connection they had that first gut level  connection with him basically he's our  guy he's going to punch the  establishment in the nose but then when  you ask them about policy about the  economy about what he's done well what  they think about Congress they've  connected to what he's saying at a  policy level you may do there may be  debate about the specific policy views  they hold but they are not just making  that cultural connection and then  dropping it there they're still with him  as he makes the case for various pieces  of policy I like Charlie's word there  this group is fascinating because you  said that you you see supporters saying  yeah he lies he makes things up he needs  to be more presidential yet he's still  our guy on the other hand you have the  approval rating he has the lowest  approval rating of any president at any  time how do you explain the difference  between how a gas is core group of  people who support Donald Trump in  others who say look he's doing a bad job  well it's big and important in the  controlling party in the party that  controls Congress in the White House  right now so in the country and this is  the tension Republicans are fighting  with we saw this week in the election is  they have a party in which the president  has a very strong relationship with his  core and that core has a very low  opinion about Congress and they are the  ones who vote in off your elections and  so they're gonna matter in the elections  of 2018 so people in the Republican  Party can't distance themselves from  this core on the other hand this Court  does not represent the entire country  and in fact the behavior of the  president that excites some of his core  supporters has also we saw this week in  Virginia excited the other team and kept  them not only turning on on Election Day  but getting people to run for office and  that's the tension in the Republican  Party right now but when you talk to  these voters particularly if you're in  Congress you have to worry you can't  underestimate how happy they are with  the economy though oh my gosh they're  all very happy even if they say it  doesn't directly affect them they love  the fact that the markets doing well  thank you John Davis you can see more of  the focus group Sunday morning on Face  the Nation plus John speaks with  independent Vermont senator Bernie  Sanders and Republican senator Tim Scott  of South Carolina that's Sunday on CBS

